        "CO2 Emission Case Study\n",
        "\n",
        "Problem Statement : Your role is that of an Automotive Data Analyst. By exploring the data, uncovering hidden patterns,\n",
        "and building a predictive model, you will help the council decide which vehicle design and policy\n",
        "levers have the greatest impact on reducing emissions. The end goal is a regression model that\n",
        "accurately estimates CO₂ emissions from vehicle features and a set of actionable recommendations.\n",
        "\n",
        "This case study explores the predictive modeling of CO₂ emissions from vehicles, highlighting key diagnostic steps, feature importance, and actionable recommendations. The following sections summarize the main findings:\n",
        "\n",
        "Approach I have followed:\n",
        "1. Tried to understand the data with .info(), .describe(), .head(). No nulls initially\n",
        "2. Segregated categorical columns and numerical columns\n",
        "3. In Categorical Columns , Removed clear cut unwanted features like Car Name which has no significance\n",
        "4. Modified \"Model\" feature into a new feature called \"Model_Type\" to fit into the categories given in data dictionary\n",
        "5. Transmission type is splitted into String and numerical parts to identify Transmission type and number of gears\n",
        "6. After splitting gears into a numerical column could see some nulls for around 295 rows\n",
        "7. Imputed it with Median\n",
        "8. Used these categorical columns at a later step for one hot encoing using column transformer\n",
        "9. Checked the relationship of each numerical feature with target feature for Lienarity check\n",
        "10. Checked VIF score and realized that Engine Size, CYlinders are strongly correlated and one can be ignored\n",
        "11. Also Fuel Consumption City , Highway, Hybrid , out of these combined consumption is enough\n",
        "12. Now before giving to column transformer - Created Numerical features list and categorical features list\n",
        "13. Created input and output ( X and y respectively)\n",
        "14. Did the test_train_split using scikit learn. Goal is to avoid exposure of test data in any way\n",
        "15. Now its time for column transformer . Categorical columns are one hot encoded, numerical columns are scaled with StandardScaler()\n",
        "16. Now created a pipeline to do steps sequentially .\n",
        "17. Done a Ridge Regression with an initial alpha of 1.0\n",
        "18. Cross validation of k = 5\n",
        "19. Calculated Mean Square error and R-Square (coefficient of determination)\n",
        "20. Validated the predicted vs Actual values during plot\n",
        "\n",
        "Some Analysis observations:\n",
        "The Multicollinearity Trap:\n",
        "\n",
        "Blindly including all numerical features can destabilize the model. Fuel consumption variables (City, Highway, Combined) are highly correlated, with some being mathematical inverses. Correlation heatmaps and VIF diagnostics confirmed redundancy. To ensure reliability, we simplified by retaining only Fuel Consumption Combined (L/100 km) as the primary predictor\n",
        "\n",
        "Fuel Type Comparison:\n",
        "\n",
        "Grouped visualizations revealed systematic differences in emissions across fuel categories. Gasoline and diesel vehicles consistently showed higher CO₂ emissions, while hybrids and electric vehicles were significantly lower. However, these differences are partly confounded by vehicle class and engine size, indicating that fuel type interacts with design characteristics.\n",
        "\n",
        "Which Feature Matters More?:\n",
        "\n",
        "Correlation analysis suggested strong relationships between emissions and features like engine size and cylinders. However, Ridge regression coefficients highlighted that Fuel Consumption Combined dominates predictive importance, while Engine Size and Cylinders contribute minimally once consumption is included. This demonstrates that correlation alone can be misleading, and model coefficients provide a more accurate measure of feature importance.\n",
        "\n",
        "Outlier Vehicles:\n",
        "\n",
        "Residual analysis identified vehicles with large prediction errors. Outliers included electric vehicles, special-purpose vehicles, and extreme engine sizes. These cases reveal where linear model assumptions break down, suggesting the need for separate treatment or non-linear modeling for niche categories.\n",
        "\n",
        "From Model to Recommendation:\n",
        "\n",
        "The model's findings translate into actionable policy and design recommendations:\n",
        "Efficiency mandates: Focus on reducing combined fuel consumption.\n",
        "Fleet composition policies: Address disproportionate emissions from SUVs and pickups.\n",
        "Fuel incentives: Encourage adoption of EVs and hybrids through targeted incentives.\n",
        "Grounding recommendations in model evidence ensures they are data-driven and impactful.\n",
        "\n",
        "Visual Validation:\n",
        "\n",
        "Beyond metrics, visual validation confirmed model quality. Predicted vs actual plots showed points closely aligned with the reference line, while residual plots revealed mostly random scatter. Slight under-prediction at high emission levels suggests potential non-linearity, but overall the model demonstrates strong accuracy and reliability.\n",
        "\n",
        "RECOMMENDATIONS as per the analysis done:\n",
        "Target Efficiency Mandates -->>\n",
        "Since Fuel Consumption Combined is the strongest driver, policies should focus on reducing average consumption across fleets.\n",
        "Examples: stricter efficiency standards, incentives for lightweight designs.\n",
        "\n",
        "Address Vehicle Class Contributions -->>\n",
        "SUVs and pickups consistently push emissions higher.\n",
        "Recommendations: encourage smaller vehicle adoption, regulate fleet composition, or impose higher taxes on high-emission classes.\n",
        "\n",
        "Promote Cleaner Fuel Types -->>\n",
        "Electric and hybrid vehicles show dramatically lower emissions.\n",
        "Recommendations: expand EV infrastructure, provide purchase subsidies, and incentivize hybrid adoption.\n",
        "\n",
        "Handle Outlier Categories Separately -->\n",
        "EVs, special-purpose vehicles, and extreme engine sizes behave differently from mainstream combustion vehicles.\n",
        "Consider separate models or tailored policies for these niche categories."
